ie 100 well both  of those came off by more than 20% at  the open they stabilized during the last  hour or so but both of those stocks very  very important bellwethers held by  millions of shareholders around the  world by almost every index track of  funds will those of fallen but they're  around about 17 to 18 percent lower  right  now the other really dramatic news that  I have to bring you Adam is what's been  going on in the gilt market now you've  obviously seen a big dash towards  sovereign debt bonds in recent days and  that has depressed the yield to the  yield falls when the price rises and you  saw on Friday afternoon for example on  Wall Street US Treasuries really the  yield on those really really dropping  overnight you've seen that yield on  30-year US Treasuries the long bond  which is a very key financial instrument  that's gone below 1% for the first time  ever this morning more dramatically that  not for the UK UK government bonds gilts  you've seen the yield on 2-year gilts go  negative for the first time ever in  other words borrowers are actually  paying the UK government for the right  to lend a mother to lend money to it now  this is something we've seen in the  eurozone over the years for example with  German government bonds you've seen  those trading on negative yields this  has never happened with the UK before  say very very dramatic moment all sorts  of implications Adam for government  borrowing given him that rishis soon at  the new chancellor is being urged in  some quarters to increase government  borrowing turn on the spending taps and  the gilt market essentially certainly on  the 2-year level is giving him every  opportunity to do that but I mean the  broad picture Adam is it's absolute  carnage you're seeing that as I say big  big drops in some of the oil prices the  likes of International Airlines Group  the owner of British Airways that's been  smacked by another 8% today as well so  really ugly picture right across the  piece and the irony in King is that  normally a low oil price and low  interest rates would be a stimulus for  industry yes that's absolutely right out  of normally a higher or price is seen as  a tax on growth but in this particular  instance you've got a fairly toxic  combination because the oil price has  got two factors depressing that one is  lower demand as a result of coronavirus  and the other is higher supply with the  Saudis turning on the spending taps this  this is going to have all sorts of  knock-on effects everywhere Adam you're  going to see for example I think some of  the big US shale producers they're going  to come under pressure a lot of those  have issued a lot of debt as in the junk  bond market you can see that that coming  under pressure so the knock on the  we'll be right across all sorts of  financial assets classes but to your  point is very interesting actually in  this regard to look at what happened in  Japan overnight now the Nikkei the main  Japanese stock index fell by some 5%  Japan is a very very big oil consumer a  big oil importer other things being  equal you would assume that this would  do this would be very good for the  Japanese economy potentially offer some  stimulus what seems to have happened is  that there's been a rush into the  Japanese yen now the yen is obviously  seen as a bit of a safe haven at times  like this a higher yen means that it's  more expensive for Japanese exporters to  export their goods and accordingly  that's why you've seen the Nikkei  selling off you're seeing all sorts of  asset classes coming under some very  unusual pricing movements indeed
 

